                between the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
                and
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_3
                correspond to the two factors of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(x_2,x_2)
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponent1.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %p
                This way each sub-graph encodes its corresponding subset of factors in the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentTwo.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentThree.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %blockquote
                %p
                  graphics grabbed from
                  %a{:href => "Feynman+diagram#Brouder10"} Brouder 10
              %p
                A priori this
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
                is defined away from coincident vertices:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo ≠
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \neq x_j
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i = x_j
                requires a choice of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
                locus. This choice is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re-")normalization
                of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
          %h3#as_a_zeta_function As a zeta function
          %blockquote
            %p needs harmonization
          %p
            From another perspective, the loop contributions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            are typically would-be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traces"} traces
            over inverse powers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{-n}
            of the relativistic particle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
          %p
            For instance for the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+particle"} scalar particle
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            in 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            the 1-loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+amplitude"} vacuum amplitude
            is the regularized trace over the Feynman propagator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∝
                  %mo ∫
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 4
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %msup
                        %mi m
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\propto \int d^4 \mathbf{p} \; \frac{1}{\mathbf{p}^2 - m^2}
          %p
            where the integral would naively be over all of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            %em Feynman propagator
            or
            %em propagator
            for short (e.g.
            %a{:href => "#Grozin05"} Grozin 05, section 2.1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kleinert11"} Kleinert 11, 8.1
            %em
              %a{:href => "Feynman+diagram#ForFinitelyManyDegreesOfFreedom"} Feynman diagram – For finitely many degrees of freedoms
            for how this comes about.
          %p
            Several methods are considered for
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regularization+%28physics%29"} regularizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+regularization"} zeta function regularization
            (also “analytic regularization/renormalization”
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Speer71"} Speer 71
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+an+elliptic+differential+operator"} zeta function
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+operator"} wave operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = \mathbf{p}^2 + m^2
            is well-defined for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(s) \gt 1
            by the naive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Tr
                    %mi reg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat \zeta_H(s)\coloneqq Tr_{reg}( H^{-s} )
          %p
            and defined from there by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+continuation"} analytic continuation
            on allmost all of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+values+of+L-functions"} special value
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s = 1
            (or its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+value"} principal value
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BCEMZ03"} BCEMZ 03, section 2.4.2
            %a{:href => "#Grozin05"} Grozin 05, section 2.1
          %p
            Representing the (completed)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"} zeta function
            here are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mellin+transform"} Mellin transform
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+function"} theta function
            – which in the present case is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Tr
                    %mi reg
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi t
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\mapsto Tr_{reg} \exp(-t H)
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            of the given theory, is what in the physics literature is known as the
            = succeed "-formulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger+parameter"} Schwinger parameter
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Tr
                    %mi reg
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi s
                  %mo =
